WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.720
Three, one.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:43.670
Quest World was a place in the computers, it was sort of an idea of a cyberspace. It's

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:47.930
what a full cyberspace world would look like. It was sort of a place of the mind they could

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.390
go to work out problems.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:55.620
It's sort of like when we play a video game, we imagine ourselves in the virtual world

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.000
of the video game. It's, you know, and we're in control. And that's really what, I guess,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.040
world was for Johnny Quest. It was not just being inside the video game, being

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.440
inside this computer world, but it was reality. You could actually defeat real

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.220
villains while being in this universe.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:29.410
Johnny and Haji created this virtual reality world in their own computer that they would enter and

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.980
Everything within that world would be created in CGI, and there would be a clear delineation

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:42.310
of where the 2D ends and the 3D begins and that sort of thing.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.510
Can you believe this?

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:47.510
It was initially created for themselves as games that they could play and challenge each

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:48.510
other.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.180
We call it Quest World.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.840
This is the gateway to a whole new reality.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.470
But fairly soon one of the arch-villains, CERD, would infiltrate it and it became contaminated

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.000
and there would be a lot of struggle.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.760
and challenges within that quest world so it was a whole new separate world to

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:14.600
explore all right let's hit it their world was not unlike like a James Bond

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:19.980
world where they rode on motorcycles and there's fast cars and yeah there's some

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.780
jetpacks but they're in quest world they could create stuff that's beyond that

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:30.000
that is you know when a limitation is really just her only imagination you could be a

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.490
one day in Quest World and you'd be Mr. Buff. Can you imagine that? You know, you've all the parts

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:41.180
of your personality, your weakest parts, your strongest parts, your bits you don't like. If any

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.880
one moment you were in Quest World or in virtual reality and at that particular moment, one of

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:53.470
those parts of your personality came through, that's what would project. It was sort of what

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:59.690
they would create for themselves. So they fought themselves as superheroes, as bigger, stronger,

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:00.000
fast.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.440
or whatever, they can.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:09.560
It was going to be a combination of CG and some 2D animation.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.580
How those two were going to mix was a question.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.400
We didn't really know at that point

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.440
because there's still a lot of 2D animation in Quest World in that show.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.820
We weren't quite sure how that was all going to go together.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.620
Toy Story had just come out

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.440
and it really blew a lot of people away

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.000
that you could even make a feature with a computer

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.060
And so we were pretty computer-crazy at that point.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.360
We wanted to see more computer-generated animation,

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.680
and it would be a few years before the next one

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.760
would come out from Pixar or from anybody.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.430
So seeing computer animation regularly on Johnny Quest

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:47.430
was quite a thrill.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.830
Everyone wanted some full CG animation,

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.730
but I think a lot of the companies

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.810
hadn't really quite gotten used to what

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.440
it would take to produce.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.220
Once the head is constructed by the Questworld computer,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.000
it'll be a--

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.760
to put the real head together.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.960
Yes, but let us keep in mind that this process will take a long time, Johnny.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.880
Not to mention, use up a good deal of the system's memory.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.530
Not only did it need to be designed,

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.830
but the technology for producing that stuff on a weekly basis

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:23.840
wasn't exactly worked out yet.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.640
You know, it's one thing to control a drawn series

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.000
where you have schedules which have been figured out for decades now.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.480
But the CG stuff was so new and so different.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.980
Even in the best scenario, it takes two or three shows

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.620
just to kind of get in the rhythm and find the style, right?

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.420
Here we are, we would, you know, thrown into it

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.020
and you got to find it like right away.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.120
And so it was a little bit nerve wracking,

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.220
especially for the executives because we don't even,

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.450
we haven't seen the pipeline if it works.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:00.000
By the time that Quest World was developed and

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.350
embraced by the studio.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:07.130
You know, CGI techniques had moved on a little bit, they were a little bit more accessible,

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:08.810
a little bit more economical.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:13.410
It's a lot of hours because you're under tremendous pressure, but it's not work that you can spread

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:16.350
out to a lot of different people because you're all working on the same thing.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.850
It'd be sort of like five different chefs are using the same chopping board.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:25.270
Yeah, it was still so new that the executives and people in charge didn't really know what

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.540
they were going to get, what if they were going to get it in time, whether it was going

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:30.000
to make the air dates.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.080
So there's a lot of risk involved in trying these CG sections.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:48.690
They had worked up sheets and sheets of design samples, not just of the characters, but also

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:53.090
of their toys and the guns and all the kind of things that would majorly figure into the

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:54.600
show.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:00.000
When you draw characters in normal 2D animation, you really don't draw any volume to figure

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.360
faces. They're essentially just outline. They have no volume. So when you're building

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:11.140
a CG model, it's all about defining volume. The drawing just tells you what volume you

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:15.780
need to imagine. It takes a long time to get to that point where you actually have a character

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:21.350
that can work coherently. But when you do, it's actually fairly easy to animate it.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.260
The motorcycle sequence was, I think, the first big one that we did.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.820
It was picked out because it was fairly easy to do.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.780
Because someone on a motorcycle, there's a little bit of motion,

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.450
but you don't really see a lot.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:45.870
You don't want to start off with the hardest thing you can do, obviously.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:52.620
Putting together a CG animated character is really complex.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:54.570
That first show that we put together,

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:58.530
we didn't have the CGI Johnny ready to go at that point.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.000
So we had to go with the...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.000
2D animation in Quest World in that show.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.510
The reason that his face is cell animated is very simple.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:07.910
It wasn't ready at the time.

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.350
But it seemed to work out pretty well.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:10.890
Everyone really liked that first show

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:20.250
that we put together.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:24.330
Funny thing about CG animation is

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.120
that old CG animation really looks old.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.180
Now, between you and me, they look pretty funky,

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.000
but I think it's--

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.000
real time capsule of where CG was at the time. It gave it an extra visual edge, and particularly

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.240
in its day, it was definitely something different, something that no other show had.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:47.660
It wasn't as easy to do as it would have been now. Nowadays, we can just do a lot more quicker.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.420
We have much faster computers. The software is, generally speaking, more sophisticated and

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:57.670
efficient. We spend a lot of time and money doing things that nowadays only take a quarter

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:58.870
that amount of time.

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:00.000
If we were to

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.640
If you update Johnny Quest again, Questworld would be kind of an open forum for everyone

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:12.160
to contribute and be kind of like this afterlife situation where everyone from all over the

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.180
world can contribute, create their own characters and own situation and that they would be part

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.180
of it.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.170
I mean obviously it would be much more sophisticated now because you know you can achieve much

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.050
more with a lot more CGI, a lot more 3D.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:29.200
But I think what we would have done is to have used those techniques to explore a lot

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:30.000
more kind of

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.800
psychological drama. I can imagine someday some future Johnny Quest where the

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.580
whole show is in motion capture CG and then they go into Quest World and that

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:54.220
part is done in Alex Toth artwork. You know that would be really cool. I think

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:57.880
for the most part it worked quite well. It was really Hanna-Barbera and Time

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:00.000
Warner's really first try-a-trick

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.620
doing a lot of CGI on a TV schedule.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.300
There's things that could have been better

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.180
and there's things that probably could have been worse.

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.400
It came out nice, it was a good effort,

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.040
and I think it was pretty well received at the time.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.380
Considering what we had available to do it,

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:16.040
I think we did pretty good.
